nto separate helper functions like `handleQuote` and `handleClaim`—this would make the intent clearer and reduce the complexity of `runScenario` itself, even though it adds function invocations. Clarity wins out here. Calculating code mass... Finishing the assignment and closing out the conditional logic. Now I'm reconsidering the refactoring approach—extracting `handleQuote` and `handleClaim` functions would improve clarity and intent, though it might add a bit of mass through extra function bindings. The clarity benefit under Rule 2 seems worth the tradeoff, but I'm weighing whether the guidance to minimize mass should take precedence here.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Baseline is green (42 tests) and tsc-clean. Now I'll refactor `src/cli.ts`.\n\n## Refactoring Analysis of `src/cli.ts`\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n- `readStdin` — clearly reveals intent (reads stdin). Keep.\n- `runScenario` — clearly reveals intent (runs a scenario, returns results). Keep.\n- `main` — conventional entry-point name. Keep.\n- **Issue found**: inside `runScenario`, the `forEach` callback packs two distinct responsibilities (quote handling and claim handling) plus dispatch logic into one dense block. The step-index-as-policy-id relationship and the mutation of `quoteCount` are buried. Intent would be revealed better by extracting focused handlers.\n\n### Step 2: Initial APP Mass — `runScenario`\n\nCounting the callback body of the `forEach`:\n- Constants: `\"quote\"`, `0`, `1`, `\"claim\"` template strings (~5) = 5\n- Bindings: `customer`, `steps`, `policies`, `quoteCount`, `results`, `step`, `index`, `premium`, `policy`, `result` (~10) = 10\n- Invocations: `new Map`, `quote`, `set`, `insuranceSum`, `*`, `push`, `get`, `Error`, `!`, `claim`, `push`, `Error` (~12) = 24\n- Conditionals: `if quote`, `else if claim`, `if !policy`, `else` (4) = 16\n- Loops: `forEach` (1) = 5\n- Assignments: `quoteCount += 1`, `policy.remainingCap = ...` (2) = 12\n\n**Total Mass ≈ 72**\n\n### Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n- **Rule 1 (Tests pass)**: green baseline confirmed.\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals intent)**: extract `handleQuoteStep` / `handleClaimStep` so `runScenario` reads as a clean dispatch loop. The `quoteCount` mutation is awkward; a `switch` over `step.op` also makes the exhaustive dispatch clearer.\n- **Rule 3 (No duplication)**: both branches call `results.push`; that repetition can be lifted so each handler returns a `Result` and the loop pushes once.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest elements per unit)**: smaller focused functions.\n\n### Step 4: Implement"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_012yFGrz2ug6E368z4wahDcH","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_15-50-57_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_opus-4-8-no-thinking/src/cli.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"type PolicyState = { items: Item[]; remainingCap: number };\n\nconst runScenario = (scenario: Scenario): Result[] => {\n  const { customer, steps } = scenario;\n  const policies = new Map<number, PolicyState>();\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  const results: Result[] = [];\n\n  steps.forEach((step, index) => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const premium = quote(customer, step.items, {\n        contractIndex: quoteCount,\n      });\n      quoteCount += 1;\n      policies.set(index, {\n        items: step.items,\n        remainingCap: CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(step.items),\n      });\n      results.push({ premium });\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      const policy = policies.get(step.policy);\n      if (!policy) {\n        throw new Error(`Claim references unknown policy: ${step.policy}`);\n      }\n      const result = claim(policy.items, step.incident, policy.remainingCap);\n      policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push({\n        payout: result.payout,\n        remainingCap: result.remainingCap,\n      });\n    } else {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown operation: ${(step as { op: string }).op}`);\n    }\n  });\n\n  return results;\n};","newText":"type PolicyState = { items: Item[]; remainingCap: number };\n\ntype QuoteContext = {\n  customer: Scenario[\"customer\"];\n  contractIndex: number;\n};\n\nconst registerQuote = (\n  policies: Map<number, PolicyState>,\n  policyId: number,\n  step: QuoteStep,\n  context: QuoteContext,\n): Result => {\n  const premium = quote(context.customer, step.items, {\n    contractIndex: context.contractIndex,\n  });\n  policies.set(policyId, {\n    items: step.items,\n    remainingCap: CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(step.items),\n  });\n  return { premium };\n};\n\nconst settleClaim = (\n  policies: Map<number, PolicyState>,\n  step: ClaimStep,\n): Result => {\n  const policy = policies.get(step.policy);\n  if (!policy) {\n    throw new Error(`Claim references unknown policy: ${step.policy}`);\n  }\n  const result = claim(policy.items, step.incident, policy.remainingCap);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return { payout: result.payout, remainingCap: result.remainingCap };\n};\n\nconst runScenario = (scenario: Scenario): Result[] => {\n  const { customer, steps } = scenario;\n  const policies = new Map<number, PolicyState>();\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n\n  return steps.map((step, policyId) => {\n    switch (step.op) {\n      case \"quote\":\n        return registerQuote(policies, policyId, step, {\n          customer,\n          contractIndex: quoteCount++,\n        });\n      case \"claim\":\n        return settleClaim(policies, step);\n      default:\n        throw new Error(`Unknown operation: ${(step as { op: string }).op}`);\n    }\n  });\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":8417,"output":3903,"cacheRead":9704,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":22024,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784910126932,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl