uirements, so I'll go with that approach.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 12 tests pass. Now let me perform the refactoring analysis.\n\n## Refactoring Analysis\n\n**Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)**\n\n- `basePremiumForItem` — clearly reveals intent (base premium for a single item). ✓ Keep.\n- `itemSurcharges` — plural name, returns the summed surcharges for one item. Fits intent. ✓ Keep.\n- `quote`, `mainItemsBasePremium`, `componentsBasePremium`, `countByType`, `isComponent`, `componentGroupPremium` — all clear and descriptive. ✓ Keep.\n\nNo renames needed; names already reveal intent.\n\n**Step 2: Current APP Mass of the recently-added `itemSurcharges`**\n\n```ts\nconst itemSurcharges = (item: Item): number => {\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += basePremiumForItem(item) * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  return surcharge;\n};\n```\n\n- Constants: `0` = 1\n- Bindings: `item`, `surcharge` = 2\n- Invocations: `basePremiumForItem()`, `*` = 4\n- Conditionals: `if` = 4\n- Assignments: `let surcharge = 0`, `surcharge +=` = 12\n\n**Total Mass: 23**\n\n**Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules**\n\n- Rule 1 (Tests Pass): ✓ all green.\n- Rule 2 (Reveals Intent): The mutable accumulator (`let ... +=`) implies multiple accumulating surcharges, but only one exists. A direct expression reads more clearly *for the current behavior*.\n- Rule 3 (No Duplication): No duplication.\n- Rule 4 (Fewest Elements): The `let`/`+=` accumulator is speculative structure for future surcharges (YAGNI). Can be simplified to a single expression.\n\n**Step 4: Implement Refactoring** — convert the mutable accumulator to a pure ternary expression."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_015RH5zFCZsi3YDMxY5Rg9RY","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const itemSurcharges = (item: Item): number => {\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += basePremiumForItem(item) * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  retur